opitulemused_en:
- Student who has passed the course
- 1)	 knows the cultural differences and can analyze how culture affects people's behavior and psychological processes;
- 2) has the awareness (and ability) to adjust in a cross-cultural environment;
- 3) knows cross-cultural research methods and issues in cross-cultural measurement.

- At the end of the course, the students should be able to:
- 1) know the formation of genital tract microbiota and its composition;
- 2) understand factors influencing genital tract microbiota;
- 3) know causative agents of genital tract infections and describe pathogenesis of the diseases;
- 4) choose optimal diagnostic methods in case of genital tract infections;
- 5) know the treatment principles of genital tract infections.

- 1) is well-informed about different forms of business and can choose the most appropriate one for one's own idea;
- 2) can analyse the processes of business;
- 3) can analyse businessplan and different forms of enterprises.

- By the end of the course the student:
- - knows the grammatical relations of constituents;
- - has an overview of the parts of speech in Estonian, can identify them in a sentence;
- - knows the main sentence types of Estonian (normal sentence, existential sentence, possessive clause, experiential clause, resultative clause), can form and characterize them;
- - can differentiate between different sentence types (full, defective and elliptical sentence, sentence with homogenous parts, compound sentence, complex sentence) and characterize them;
- - can use Estonian punctuation characters in different sentence types;
- - knows non-finite constructions and their functions, can form sentences with different non-finite constructions;
- - can apply and explain the rules of agreement;
- - knows and can apply the main rules of word order use in Estonian.

- After completing the course, the student
- 1) is able to diagnose more common urological pathologies;
- 2) has an understanding of the performing of diagnostic procedures ad their indications;
- 3) has general comprehension of contemporary treatment principles of urological diseases.

- By the end of the course a student will be able to:
- 1. define multimedia basic terms;
- 2. choose basic components of multimedia hardware and outline features of various models;
- 3. use raster graphics for editing pictures;
- 4. use 2D and 3D graphics for creating and editing images;
- 5. create, save and process videos and audio clips;
- 6. create simple animations.

- After the course, the students are expected:
- 1.	to understand the quantum nature of matter and to be familiar with the respective concepts and terminology;
- 2.	to know the difference between the atoms, molecules and bulk matter, and to be able to describe their fundamental structural differences;
- 3.	to be acquainted with basic spectroscopic techniques of studying matter;
- 4.	to be able to analyse different phases of matter and transitions between them;
- 5.	to know the laws of thermodynamics;
- 6.	to understand the following concepts of themodynamics: energy, work, heat, temperature, entropy etc.;
- 7.	to understand life as the self-organized process of high-quality (low-entropy) energy utilization;
- 8.	to know the enery sources of biosphere and the cell;
- 9.	to be able to use the acquired knowledge and skills in the context of forthcoming biological disciplines.

- After passing the subject student will:
- be acquainted in evidence-based medical communication techniques based on motivational interviewing and the Calgary-Cambridge model;
- understand the impact of attitudes and stereotypes on the quality of communication, on the emergence and resolution of conflicts and medical errors;
- apply evidence-based communication techniques in the course of consultation, team-work and conflict resolution in predetermined contingency tasks;
- is familiar with the specifics of healthcare equality for different groups and the main pathways on how socio-demographic characteristics are related to health.

- Competences obtained by the student:
- 1. knows main concepts related to digital technologies in art;
- 2. is able to use digital tecnologies in art;
- 3. is able to teach and advise pupils in using digital technologies in art.

- After passing the course student
- Upon successful completion of this course, students should:
*	conceive of folklore as a tradition-based communicative practice and have developed an in-depth understanding of its differences from (intangible) cultural heritage; 
*	have a good command of the concept of genre in folkloristics;
*	be capable of recognising opportunities for using vernacular expressions in community work, identity policy, cultural industry and tourism;
*	be prepared to act as mediators between different actors in the fields of cultural heritage, including communities, entrepreneurs and officials; 
*	have improved their skills in assessing their own professional activities and those of others, in working in teams and in expressing their professional views to different interests groups orally and in writing.

