opitulemused_en:
- The student
- - understands the theoretical, practical and ethical principles by which museums and their collections can and should be managed;
- - critically applies the relevant theoretical framework and practical tools to actual situations in the museum and cultural heritage protection institutions environment;
- - obtains skills to operate with museum information systems;
- - has effective time management skills necessary to complex work environment, is able to make independent choices within the framework of one's internship program;
- - has team work experience;
- - self/critically reflects upon his/her work and learning experience.

- After passing the course student
- Upon successful completion of this course, students should:
*	be familiar with the main theories of cultural heritage and know how to use and combine them; 
*	gain an overview of opportunities and challenges related to political, economic and societal applications of cultural heritage;
*	be capable of linking contemporary cultural processes with the workings of cultural policies;
*	know how to put knowledge obtained in the course into practice in individual and team work.

- After completing this course, the student:
- - is familiar with more common theories and everyday concepts of information society, both on the macro and micro level;
- - is familiar with the vocabulary and has the skills to seek further information on concepts in information society and media studies;
- - is able to discuss and critically analyse some of the contemporary issues relating to information society.

- After completing the course the student:
- 1. has the basic knowledge of the first half of the 19th century Russian literature, knows the concept of romanticism and its main characteristics;
- 2. understands the cultural and historical context of Russian romanticism;
- 3. knows the top writers of the period and their creation;
- 4. analyzes the texts of Russian romanticism,
- 5. systematizes the acquired knowledge in the preparation of the exam.

- Passing through the module, a participant
- Knows net and gross premiums;
- Can estimate cash-flows (fund development);
- Knows net and gross premium reserves;
- Knows multiple state models in life insurance: translation probabilities, rates, actuarial present values.

- After completion of the course the student:
1. Is familiar with and able to use the basic terminology of clinical pharmacology and evidence based medicine; able to find, use and critically appraise drug information;
2. Is able to decide if pharmacotherapy is necessary, pose a measurable treatment goal, select a drug group and a drug based on efficacy, safety and cost, consider suitability of the chosen therapy to the patient, Is able to recognise an adverse drug reaction and to manage this, write a correct prescription, inform the patient and monitor the treatment.

- The student will be expected:
- - to understand and explain the organization of public health and health care systems in different countries;
- - to explain the aim and implementation of reforms of health services and health care systems and to analyze these in the global context;
- - to understand the basics of health and economic impact assessment in clinical medicine and public health.

- The student 1) is acquainted with basic knowledge of the function of organs and organ systems;
2) is able to describe the role of the nervous system in the functioning of the organism as a whole;
3) is able to explain the role of the hormonal system in combining different organs and organ systems into a whole;
4) is able to analyse interconnections between the structure and function of an organism;
5) comprehends the cooperation of different organs in movement activity.

- After passing the course the student:
1) reflects major microbial technologies for treatment of polluted water, air and soil;
2) has an overview of biological treatment technologies for organic wastes, production of energy by microbially mediated processes and biomining;
3) is able to make environmentally sustainable choices in his work and daily life;
4) is able to apply the knowledge of environmental biotechnology in planning the company's waste management.
5) is able to analyze the existing and design novel environmentally sustainable processes and technologies.

- On completion of this module the individual
- 1)	understands the basic accounting terms and concepts, the role of financial and management accounting;
- 2)	knows the basic methods of assets, liabilities and owners' equity accounting;
- 3)	knows how to use financial ratios analyzing the annual reports of the company;
- 4)	is able to solve the typical management accounting exercises.

- By the end of the course the student
- 1) can summarize texts of journalistic and academic content, find information in the text;
- 2) can convey in text the statistical data presented in graphs and tables;
- 3) can write a summary of an academic article;
- 4) can compile and deliver a presentation based on a problem-solution article;
- 5) can participate in a discussion in Estonian and lead a discussion.

- A Student
- - knows how to pronounce Latin words (according to the rules of Ancient and Medieval Latin);
- - is generally familiar with the grammatical structure, morphology and syntax of the Latin language;
- - knows the principal forms of nouns, adjectives and verbs in Latin;
- - distinguishes between the principal forms of nouns from the 1st  to the 5th declension and adjectives from the 1st  to the 3rd declension, as well as their forms of comparison;
- - recognizes the case, number and gender of nouns, adjectives and pronouns
- as well as the verbal forms in the present, imperfect  and perfect tenses  in active voice,   is familiar with the principles of using prepositions;
- - is familiar with the basic vocabulary in Latin;
- - is able to make use of the dictionaries of Latin;
- - translates simpler Latin texts with the help of a dictionary.

- After passing the course student
- - will know animals that live in different habitats and can see their similarities and differences;
- - can relate their characteristic features to the conditions of their habitats;
- - can understand the importance of animals as part of the ecological system;
- - will know the  topics as well as skills and attitudes set  in the national curriculum in the field of nature studies; will develop methodological skills in teaching nature-related topics

hindamise_meetod:
Course assessment

The prerequisites for positive result are:
1. 100% participation in (auditory) seminars;
2. to listen all e-lectures; 
3. to conduct all lecture tests (self-control tests) at a maximum score (100%) in the Moodle, the number to perform tests is not limited; 
4. to visit family practice centre and complete observation diary;
5. to perform the group-work presentation for the seminar "Upper respiratory tract infections";
6. to perform the practical assignment in the practical skills seminar;
7. The final test in the Moodle`i consists of 10 questions and cover both lecture and seminar materials. It must be performed at least 70% score. The number of performance is one time. The time for taking the test is 20 minutes and it is pre determined (see the schedule of your group) and the test cannot be taken at another time. You take the test at home one time during the given time.

The final grade is transferred to ÕIS the day after the final exam.

The first re-evaluation takes place at a pre-arranged time, usually one week after the first final exam. For this purpose, all work that was not submitted for regular evaluation or that did not meet the requirements must be submitted and the final test with a negative result must be retaken.

The second re-evaluation takes place at the end of the semester.

