opitulemused_en:
- After passing the course student
- The student acquires following skills and knowledge:
Knowledge of important theorems in public finance;
Application conditions when using these theorems;
Knowledge of vocabulary in public finance;
Methods of analysis: graphical, algebraic and verbal argumentation;
Application of microeconomic and macroeconomic theory in Public Economics;
Experience in solving theoretical problems by developing broader skills to work with the scientific literature in this field;
Knowledge how to assess criticaly the applicability of different theoretical models;
Knowledge about the empirical evidence in applying different public policies

- After passing the course student
- A student who has completed the course:

    communicates in Estonian at their language level;

    actively uses the acquired vocabulary;

    initiates conversation when necessary, knows how to ask questions in various communication situations, and respond to questions addressed to them.

- After passing this course, the student should  
* know how to apply statistical methodology for various real-life problems; 
* be able to choose an appropriate statistical method to solve a practical problem; 
* know the concept of reproducible data analysis, its importance, and be able to use tools (knitr, markdown) to carry it out; 
* be able to visualise the results of the analysis effectively (specifically, use package ggplot2 for creating static graphs and package shiny to add interactivity);
* be able to choose a suitable method to visualise results and justify the method selection.

- After passing the course student
- Upon completing the course, a student:
*  knows the international moot-court competitions, which are most well known in Tartu University and is able to pick the competition most suitable with the students skills and career choices; 
*  understands the main benefits of mooting and which skills participation in the moot develops;
* knows the basic requirements of drafting written pleadings in moot courts;
* knows the main databases of cases and academic journals relevant for international moot-courts;
* knows the basic principles of pleading before a moot court panel.

- Student taking the course will understand:
- 1)	the current trends and the role of management accounting on the planning and analyses of the companies' financial performance;
- 2)	the costing methods and is able to use these methods;
- 3)	how to use cost information in decision making;
- 4)	the main principles of the master budget and performance measurement;
- 5) how to present and explain the management accounting information.

- During the practice the students develop their Estonian language skills in the linguistic environment.
- A student who has completed the course
- 1. knows and practices basic fieldwork methods;
- 2. has acquired basic knowledge of collecting and systematizing language material;
- 3. has improved his/her knowledge of the Estonian language and culture as a result of fieldwork or archival work.

- After passing the course student
- Participants will be able:
to differentiate between types of conflicts;
to give examples of different types of conflicts;
to name destructive and constructive conflict resolution ways;
to analyze conflicts
to describe communication skills needed for conflict resolution.

- Upon completing the internship, the students will:
- know standard operating procedures and best practices applied in public/private/non-governmental professional settings dealing with international relations;
- develop professionally relevant competencies and relationships in a professional setting;
- understand professional organisational culture;
- demonstrate communication skills, cognitive skills and teamwork.

- After passing the course student
- B2:
1) can understand the main ideas of complex text on both concrete and abstract topics, including technical discussions in his/her field of specialization;
2) can interact with a degree of fluency and spontaneity that makes regular interaction with native speakers quite possible without strain for either party;
3) can produce clear, detailed text on a wide range of subjects and explain a viewpoint on a topical issue giving the advantages and disadvantages of various options.
C1:
1) can understand a wide range of demanding, longer texts, and recognize implicit meaning
2) can express him/herself fluently and spontaneously without much obvious searching for expressions;
3) can use language flexibly and effectively for social, academic and professional purposes;
4) can produce clear, well-structured, detailed text on complex subjects, showing controlled use of organizational patterns, connectors and cohesive devices.

- After passing the course student
- By the end of the course a student will 
*understand the importance of teaching and learning culture in English classes; 
*have enough knowledge about history and culture of Australia, New Zealand and South Africa to teach related topics in English classes at basic school; 
*be aware of different techniques for teaching culture in English lessons at basic school; 
*design a worksheet or study aid;
*write a booklog; 
*have an overview of relevant literature and websites

- After completing the course, the student:
- - can use the basic vocabulary and grammar for the level A1.1;
- - is able to participate in the simple daily conversations;
- - can talk and write about her/himself, about, her/his day, family and hobbies.

- After passing the course student
- The student, who has taken the course has the following skills: 
- to find legal issues in complicated theoretical cases to be presented in oral arguments and critically evaluate them;
- to prepare for presentation of oral arguments;
- to present oral arguments;
- to plan group work and work in a group

- Upon successful completion of the course, the student:
- * Is able to find and analyse relevant background information about the business plan;
- * Is able to specify and analyse problems, set strategic goals, implement and manage project in terms of strategic planning, related activities, and budget;
