- After passing through the course its participants
- * can select most relevant study design;
- * can apply statistical methods to answer questions arising from biological/medical research

- Having passed the course, the successful student is expected to be improved in understanding:
- * pixels, voxels and vectors;
- * dimensionality of images;
- * convolution, inertia, convexity, eigenvectors and eigenvalues;
- * the colour spaces used for images;
- * methods compensating for non-ideal light conditions;
- * handling hardware related image distortions;
- * determining the hardware limitations of the capturing system;
- * how to build an end-to-end image processing pipeline;
- * field-specific machine learning.

- Students who have passed the course:
- - are familiar with information search principles and advanced search techniques and can apply them effectively in different search environments to find sources needed for their research;
- - are familiar with and can successfully use specific to their research field and important multidisciplinary research databases accessible via UT Library;
- - are familiar with scholarly information search environments on the Internet (Google Scholar, BASE, OAlster) and can find open access research information on the Internet;
- - can use the reference manager of their choice for managing their references, for in-text citations and for creating bibliographies;
- - have realised the importance of raw data and the necessity of data management:
- - can find and use open data repositories;
- - know what Digital Object Identification (DOI) is and how to get the DataCite DOIs for their data collections.

- By the end of the course students have to:
- - be able to explain the principles and fundamental concepts of international criminal law;
- - be aware of topical issues of international criminal law and be able to discuss them;
- - be able to orientate in the relevant institutions and instruments

- Student who has completed the course:
- * knows the effect of carbon dioxide dissolution on the aquatic environment and is able to give examples;
- * knows the composition of air and the dissolution of gases in water;
- * understands chemical equilibrium, alkalinity, dissolution and redox processes, and biodegradation;
- * calculates the contents, alkalinity and solubility of substances;
- * understands the carbon and nitrogen cycle, explains the substance cycle orally;
- * analyzes information, draws conclusions and presents them;
- * explains the environmental impact and effects of substances on humans on the basis of the assessment of the complex biodegradability of substances.

- A student who has passed:
- 1) remembers the rules of standard pronunciation and pronounces words correctly;
2) understands listening and reading texts on familiar topics and is able to convey the content heard and read in simple words;
3) compares different dictionaries and can find adequate information in them;
4) formulates different types of questions and answers them;
5) debates within the topics and communication situations covered in the course.

