- By the end of the course the student:
- - knows the main epochs of Art History, the main styles, movements, representatives and their work;
- - understands and appreciates the essence of older art history, modernism and postmodernism;
- - can make compositional and deskriptive analyses;
- - uses speciality- related terms;
- - connect theory with practical art exercises and applies this mastery his/her work.

- After passing this course the student:
- 1. Can use basic programming constructs such as variable, expression, assignment statement, conditional statement, loop, function, recursion, and data exchange with the user and files;
- 2. Gets familiar with basic data types and data structures in Python.
- 3. Gets familiar with basic concepts of the object-oriented paradigm (encapsulation, abstraction, inheritance, polymorphism, overloading, overriding, etc.) in Python;
- 4. Gets familiar with different types of algorithms and can assess their complexity.
- 5. Understands the workflow of a Python program, can create and correctly format programs and debug them;
- 6. Can implement entry level software projects in cooperation with other programmers.

- After completing this Master course a student
- - can describe the most important experimental and theoretical achievements that contributed to the birth of quantum mechanics (years 1887-1926);
- - can describe different formalisms and representations of quantum mechanics, explain physical contents and mathematical background of basic postulates;
- - has an adequate understanding of the relationship between classical analytical mechanics and quantum mechanics (Poisson brackets and commutation rules, trajectories in Hamilton phase space and quasiclassical approximation, momentum eigenvalue problem and the spin);
- - understands the difference between macroscopic and microscopic states of a statistical assembly, and the connection between the indistinguishability principle and Pauli exclusion principle, can derive Maxwell-Boltzmann, Fermi-Diraci anda Bose-Einstein distributions, and explain the phenomenon of Bose-Einstein condensation;
- - knows the basic terms of the scattering theory - scattering matrix, phase shift, scattering amplitude and cross-section, Levinson theorem, scattering length, Born approximation;
- - can describe tunnel and resonance effects, calculate transparency and reflectivity of a potential barrier, apply transfer matrix method;
- - has an idea of the methods of solution of the inverse Schrödinger problem (or the inverse scattering problem), and the related applications, knows what is soliton.

- At the end of the course the students are able to:
- explain the nature of the main institutes of the law and nature of the general principles and relations between them;
- understand the different branches of the law and the specific of their application;
- apply different methods of interpretation in order to explain the content and the aim of different legal regulations; 
- to apply different norm of legal regulations for persons with special social protection.

- The student who has passed the subject:
- 1. Is familiar with the teaching methodology of problem-based learning;
- 2. Is able to use background information in the task of solving the problem;
- 3. Analyzes the choice of one specific curriculum specialty in one's own interests;
- 4. Works successfully in the group;
- 5. Is able to be more tolerant towards different opinions;
- 6. Is able to justify and defend one's views by participating in the democratic discussion;
- 7. Can identify interdisciplinary connections in the curriculum;
- 8. Is aware of the activities of different working groups related to his/her curriculum, is able to choose a research topic suitable for him/her; is aware of different career options;
- 9. Is able to make decisions about the necessity of future research/work based on the topicality of environmental problems;
- 10. Can anticipate, if necessary prevent/resolve disagreements in a small working group;
- 11. Can plan his/her time;
- 12. Acquires skills for critical analysis in scientific and everyday texts;
- 13. Is able to compile and present a scientific report correctly and comprehensibly, is aware of standard errors;
- 14. Can critically analyze one's own (and others') time planning, shortcomings in finding information, and progress in solving problems.

- After the course, the student will:
- 1) apply their knowledge of physics to describe, explain and predict natural phenomena to level I and II students;
- 2) conduct experiments to demonstrate and explain physical phenomena;
- 3) plan experimental inquiry and ask questions related to the observed phenomena;
- 4) see the value of using hands-on experiments to explain natural phenomena.

- After passing the course student
- By the end of the course, the student:
- knows the history of design and can evaluate a designer`s work;
- knows the principles, elements and types of design;
- can reflect and plan his/her activities;
- is able to go through the design creative process as a group work;
- can encourage and support pupils` creativity and innovative ideas.

- Once completed the course, a student:
- navigates in different concepts of health and knows health-affecting factors in student life, is able to associate these factors with student's health and welfare;
- is able to analyse different health-related aspects and make healthy decisions;
- explains the nature and purpose of health improvement and its effect on individuals and organisation;.
- knows the principles and fields of a health-improving school and is able to create a healthy psychosocial and pysical environment;
- values up-to-date information about health and knows how to use it in daily life;
- is able to guide students in making reasonable health-related choices;
- knows different teaching techniques which follow the objectives of the national curriculum;
- navigates in health and welfare-related studies, legislature and statistics;
- knows different human organ systems, important organs and their tasks.

- 1) Has been physically active at the minimal level that has health promoting effect;
- 2) Knows how to use the software in order to monitor and analyse the workouts;
- 3) Knows possibilities how to handle with limitations to be physically active.

- -  has knowledge of the structure and function of the human body and the regularities of adaptation to movement and physical activity;
-  is acquainted with basic knowledge of the function of organs and organ systems;   
 - is able to analyse interconnections between the structure and function of an organism;
 -is able to connect different structural changes and biochemical processes occurring in the organism with motor skills and the generation or disappearance of training status and with changes in motor functions;
 - has an overview of the different methods for assessing working capacity and training status;
- comprehends the cooperation of different organs in movement activity;
- possesses knowledge on the management of an organization and systematic overview on the issues in sport movement at different levels.

- Participants of the seminar will 
- be well acquainted with the topic and problem set-up of their Master's Thesis;
- have clear vision about how to solve the problems raised  in Master's Thesis;
- have obtained additional communication skills.

- By the end of the course students
- 1) will be able to choose resources necessary for their research; 
2) will be able to analyse, generalise and interpret the results; 
3) will have acquired knowledge about compiling a thesis according to the requirements and assessment criteria of the institute; 
4) will have defended successfully their master's thesis in a preliminary defence.

- After the course student:
- 1) knows the main stages of development of Estonian culture and school education and is able to analyze their significance for modern times;
- 2) is able to use online databases of cultural history (VAU, DEA, DIGAR) and assess the source trustworthiness;
- 3) has participated in educational activities in the memory institutions and can apply this knowledge and experience in school curriculum implementation;
- 4) can apply in his/her teaching the time travel method.

- Study outcomes:
- the student knows the most recognized British authors and their work;
- the student knows different forms of children's literature and the most important British children's authors the student can analyze a literary piece considering its basic elements, the student can focus on the message;
- the student knows how to use different forms of the BCL when teaching English in the 1st and 2nd school stage, using different methods and learning strategies.

