opitulemused_en:
- After passing the course student
- After passing the course the student:
* Collects and analyses the background information needed for designing of the project and is well-informed about the regulations (environment);
* Defines and analyses problems, sets strategical objectives, carries out and manages the strategical planning of the project and business plan, coordinates and budget the activities;
* Draws up startegical plan of the project or business plan;
* Analyses and uses different methods of teamwork;
* Uses the innovation principles in developing a business;
* Organizes the evaluation and reporting of the results of the project;
* Has started as entrepreneur or has carryed out her/his project.

- After passing through the course the student
- - implements the steps of statistical data analysis when working with data;
- - is able to choose a suitable method based on the problem and data, and to justify the choice;
- - can use the chosen methods to solve the problem;
- - is able to evaluate and interpret the obtained results.

- Upon finishing the course students
- - have acquired the main terminology of syntax;
- - are able to analyse different syntactic phenomena;
- - are able to use different syntactic phenomena correctly.

- Student:
- can create tables and sort data using simple methods;
- can create and use formulas and functions;
- can produce charts

- After passing the course student
- By the end of this course, students are able to:

1)	describe, differentiate, criticise and apply major traditional and critical approaches to security;
2)	contextualise contemporary security theories vis-à-vis earlier theoretical traditions and argumentations;
3)	critically read and analyse theoretical texts as well as apply them for examining practical research questions in their own research;
4)	apply reflective skills to practical problem solving in contemporary security scene.

During the course, students will develop their: 
1)	reading skills, ability to digest and criticise theoretical arguments; 
2)	writing skills through composing a research paper; 
3)	reflective skills by critically evaluating and synthesising competing conceptions and theories of security as well as applying them to contemporary security challenges.

- After passing the course a student knows:
- 1.	basic concepts of the theory of algebraic structures (substructure, homomorphism, isomorphism, direct product);
- 2.	the constructions of factor groups and factor rings and the characterization of cyclic groups;
- 3. fundamental results about univariate and multivariate polynomials;
- 4. the proof of the fundamental theorem of algebra;
- 5.	the proof of the existence of a canonical basis of a linear transformation;
- 6.	basics of the theory of linear and bilinear functionals;
- 7.	the proof of the existence a canonical basis of a quadratic functional and is able to find a canonical form of a given quadratic form;
- 8. the basics of divisibility theory, in particular Euclidean domains.

- A student who has completed the course will know the basic concepts and be able to manipulate them, and will be able to prove and apply the basic theorems within the following topics:
- 1) signed and complex measures, Radon--Nikodym theorem (signed measure, its Hahn and Jordan derivatives and total variation; absolute continuity of a signed measure; Lebesgue and Radon--Nikodym derivatives of a signed measure; Lebesgue--Radon--Nikodym theorem; complex measures);
- 2) Radon measures (topological prerequisites -- continuous functions with compact support, Uryson's lemma and Tietze's extension theorem in a locally compact Hausdorff space L; Riesz's representation theorem for positive linear functionals on the space C_c(L,R); regularity properties of Radon measures, approximation of measurable functions with functions with compact support (Luzin's theorem) and semicontinuous functions; Riesz's representation theorem for dual spaces of the spaces C_0(L,R) and C_0(L,R); Baire measures);
- 3) differentiation of measures in the space R^n (Lebesgue differentiation theorems, change of variable formula for the Lebesgue integral in the space R^n, functions with bounded variation, Newton--Leibniz formula for the Lebesgue integral in the space R, Lebesgue--Stieltjes integrals);
- 4) L_p-spaces and their  dual spaces.

- After completing the course, the student:
- - interprets and analyzes the role of biopharmaceutics and its topics in drug development;
- - analyzes the release and dissolution of the active ingredient and the factors affecting them and evaluates their effect on the drug's pharmacokinetics, bioavailability and effect.
- - analyzes and evaluates the effect of the dosage form and administration route/method on the pharmacokinetics, bioavailability and effect of the drug;
- - can give examples, explain and analyze how the anatomical-physiological and behavioural characteristics of patients can affect the pharmacokinetics, bioavailability and effect of the drug;
- - explains the relationship between pharmacokinetics and pharmacodynamics and, by analyzing changes in pharmacokinetics, can explain possible changes in the effect of the drug;
- - can advise patients and specialists (including searching for or creating information materials) in aspects related to the administration of drugs, pointing out the factors affecting bioavailability and treatment efficiency while being able to cooperate and use appropriate professional terminology.

- After passing the course student
- By the end of the course a student
1) will know the aims and learning activities in the field of technology set for primary school in the National Curriculum ;
2) will know the aims, content and learning outcomes of art education in pre-school; 
3) will know different creativity theories and foster a child's creativity; 
4) can plan and carry out activities according to the curriculum and the age of children; 
5) will consider child development in teaching; 
6) can make samples using right techniques.

- The lecture course teaches to understand:
-  planet Earth in solar radiation;
-  spectral sensibility of human eye;
-  planetary energy balance; 
-  calculation of atmospheric transparency;
-  Forbes' effect;
-  Rayleigh and Mie scattering;
-  how the Earth's rotation influences movements;  
-  how a contemporary apartment house should look like;
-  and many other useful hints for a "green collar".

- At the end of the course, within the syllabus, a student knows and understands:
- 1. the basic knowledge about the immune system;
- 2. the cellular and molecular mechanisms of the immune system;
- 3. the general properties of the immune system related most common diseases and conditions;
- 4. how to evaluate the status of the immune system and options for immune system modulation, knows main immunological methods and their use in diagnosis;
- 5. is able to analyze the special scientific literature in immunology.

- In the end of the course participant is able to:
