opitulemused_en:
- By the end of this course:
- 1. Students will have broadened their theoretical understanding of power in International Relations to include realist, liberal, contructivist and radical perspectives;
- 2. Students will be able to put military power into context as an important aspect that shapes international politics, while also understanding that military power is just one of many important varieties of power determining relations between states;
- 3. Students will have gained detailed understanding on concepts of power beyond the mainstream IR theories to include hegemony, structural power and theories of small powers in the international system;
- 4. Students will have attained important insights into how power is wielded at different levels of the international system such as within the European Union, between the members of the United Nations Security Council, by middle powers seeking to shape international order through multilateralism, and by revolutionary political organizations;
- 5. Students will have furthered their skills in writing an analytic, argumentative scholarly research paper answering a question dealing with an important aspect of power in International Relations.

- After completing the course, students will
- explain contemporary methodologies in teaching Spanish as a foreign language (including communicative, functional, task-based, eclectic and post-method approaches) and their theoretical foundations;
- analyses learner language development, error types and the role of input/output in second language acquisition, drawing on SLA theories;
- evaluate specific teaching materials and resources (textbooks, digital tools, lesson plans, worksheets) according to learning objectives and learner profiles;
- design Spanish language lessons or instructional sequences, applying appropriate methodologies and learning activities and tasks for developing different language skills (grammar, vocabulary, pronounciation, pragmatics). Use digital tools and environments to support the teaching and learning of Spanish;
- reflect on their professional role and teacher ethics, analysing how to create a learner-centred, inclusive and motivating learning environment.

- Student:
- * understands which internal and external environmental factors influence emplyees' behaviour in an organization;
- * can analyze group dynamic and prognose the possible effect of group processes to and individual in an organization;
- * points out the cause and effect connections in organizational behavior and offers solutions for the organization to function better.

- The student who graduated the course:
- - has overview of scientific publications in biomedical science;
- - knows how to use Entrez PubMed database of scientific articles and tools;
- - orientates in medical biochemical terminology, can bring medical examples and use this knowledge for elucidating scientific problems theoretically and practically;
- - has been working as laboratory assistant in biomedical laboratory under a supervision of scientists;
- - can theoretically explain basic principles and demand for different laboratory methods used in medical biochemistry;
- - can analyze scientific information; allocate it into the biomedical/translational medical context.

- The student will have experience and knowledge in different fields of ecotechnology:
*processes taking place in surface filter systems;
*reduction of agricultural diffuse pollution by using constructed wetlands;
*practical experience in constructing/building principles of  constructed wetlands;
*building's energy requirements and efficiency;
*ecological building materials.

- After completing the course a student should be able to:
- - use the acquired vocabulary and grammar structures in everyday situations;
- - describe his or her everyday activities;
- - exchange essential information and express an opinion on familiar topics;
- - understand and use not complicated language in various communicational situations;
- - write personal letters and messages.

- After passing the course student
- After completing the course a student should be able to:
- read, write and understand simple texts in Russian;
- express oneself choosing the correct vocabulary and grammar learnt during the course;
- get into social contact (to greet, to say goodbye, to introduce oneself, to thank, to apologies etc.);
- talk about family, home town, hobbies, daily routine;  
- understand and apply basic structures of Russian grammar.

- After completing the course a student should be able to:

- understand the purpose of Russian case system;
- understand the meaning of Russian cases;
- know the contexts of usage of cases;
- apply the theoretical knowledge into practice.

- The student who has completed the course:
- - understands the essence of teamwork, incl. the main concepts and processes driving effective teamwork;
- - is more aware of his/ her contribution to the teamwork and analyze the team's effectiveness and performance.

- Students:
- are able to explain the backround of the authors and texts read in the course, analyze their style, forms of dialect used in the text;
- analyze sentences grammatically and syntactically;
- interpret the text;
- translate the text(s) of the read author with the help of dictionaries, grammars and internet resources;
- are able to find secondary sources regarding the author and text.

- -Knows how to analyse legal and ethical issues regarding privacy and data protection keeping in mind the economic, political and social implications of these fields;
-Has an understanding of the concepts, principles and regulation of privacy and personal data protection within the European and global regulatory framework;
-Knows the main legal and ethical issues related to surveillance, security, classification, discrimination, and decision autonomy in the lifecycle of data;
-Has an understanding of the differences between the legal, ethical and policy based approaches to norms and enforcement mechanisms;
-Is familiar with some technological approaches to solving legal and ethical issues in data lifecycle;
-Has an understanding of how to present their work in data science to experts from other fields.

- After passing the course student
- A student who has completed the course:
* uses Estonian grammatical cases, locative cases, and postpositions;
* conjugates verbs in the present tense and the simple past;
* compares adjectives;
* uses cardinal and ordinal numerals (1–12);
* describes their studies, daily routine, people’s appearance, and clothing.

hindamise_eeltingimused:
