- esitab kirjaliku praktikaaruande, milles analüüsib ja hindab kriitiliselt oma välitööde materjali, omandatud kogemust ja projekti raames ilmnenud eetilisi aspekte (s.h. kasutatud meetodeid ja nende rakendamist ja saadud tulemusi jm);
- esitab oma uurimuse tulemused ülikoolivälisele partnerile (kelle juures välitööd läbi viidi) sobivas vormis ja viisil. | Eeldusained: (HVKU.00.012 "Välitööd etnoloogias ja folkloristikas: meetodid ja analüüs"); (FLKU.03.035 "Etnograafiliste välitööde metodoloogia") VÕI (HVKU.03.002 "Etnograafiliste välitööde metodoloogia"); (HVKU.03.004 "Sissejuhatus rakendusantropoloogiasse") | Hindamise eeltingimused: Praktikalepingu ja praktikaruande esitamine

opitulemused_en:
- Students who have successfully completed the course,
- have an idea of the oldest Slavic alphabets;
- able to read and translate texts in Old Church Slavonic with a dictionary;
- get acquainted with the basic phonetic laws of the Proto-Slavic language;
- get an idea of the grammatical system of the Old Church Slavonic language.

- The student:
- 1) has an overview of the spread of Romance languages in the world;
- 2) has an overview of the main features and the historical background of the formation of Romance languages;
- 3) has the knowledge of the main differences of the Romance languages in respect with Latin and is able to find relations between the Romance languages.

- Upon completion of the course, the students will:
- 1) understand the theoretical foundations of qualitative and interpretive approaches to political research;
- 2) acquire the necessary knowledge and skills for designing a qualitative/interpretive research proposal, for collecting and analyzing qualitative/interpretive data, and for presenting and communicating respective findings;
- 3) be able to critically assess and analyze qualitative and interpretive studies and ensure the credibility of qualitative/interpretive research results.

- After completing the course, the student:
- - writes effective and functional texts in correct Danish;
- - is more confident and skilled in composing various texts in Danish;
- - customises their text according to the formal and stylistic requirements for the situation and the specific text;
- - expresses themselves both verbally and in writing in an academic context:
- - applies in-depth knowledge about the ortography and structure of Danish.

- On successful completion of the course the student:
- - has advanced-level knowledge in Estonian History of Thought;
- - developed skills of analysis of the texts that belong to Estonian History of Thought;
- - developed skills in academic self-expression.

- The student who has completed the course is able
- - to distinguish and describe the main types of interpretation;
- - to apply the learned memory, note-taking and interpreting techniques in an interpreting situation;
- - to demonstrate the ability to actively listen to and analyze texts of different lengths and levels of difficulty;
- - to compose different types of speeches.

- Kursuse edukalt läbinud üliõpilane:
- oskab end taani keeles nii kõnes kui kirjas arusaadavalt väljendada;
- saab aru taanikeelsest vestlusest igapäevastel teemadel;
- oskab lugeda ja mõista keskmise raskusega taanikeelseid tekste;
- omab põhiteadmisi taani keele grammatikast;
- tunneb Taani kultuuri ja ühiskonna põhijooni.

- Upon successful completion of the course, the student:
- * has necessary theoretical and methodological knowledge for writing a research paper on European History of Thought;
- * is able to conduct independent research and correctly formulate a research paper.

- Students who pass the course:
- - will be conversant in ways of presenting their work on paper and in digital form;
- - will know how to organise their artistic photos or videos and descriptions and shape them into a full and visually enjoyable overview in some form of their own choosing;
- - will know how to use computer programs to accomplish this.

- After passing the course student
- After successful completion of the course,
- you have acquired the skills of analysis of LSP-texts on science and technology in Estonian and in the foreign language;
- taking into account the text analysis results, you are able to choose the appropriate translation strategy for carrying out the translation task;
- you are able to find the necessary information for translation, to work with parallel texts and Internet databases.

- The student who completed the course:
- -	can use basic concepts, quantities and units of environmental radioactivity and dosimetry;
- -	understands interaction processes between ionizing radiation and matter, including biological effects and dose formation;
- -	knows various natural and artificial radiation sources and their applications;
- -	knows the basic safety standards, dose limits of radiation protection, and is able to apply the basic principles of radiation safety;
- -	can communicate the risks associated to ionizing radiation in a balanced way.

- A student who has completed the course:
- has in-depth knowledge of the development of the Nordic literary tradition in the 19th-21st centuries;
- is able to characterize selected genres of Nordic literature in more detail;
- is able to perform comparative analysis based on the works of different authors;
- has an overview of current research topics in the history of Nordic literature.

- After passing the course student
- Upon successful completion of the subject, the student:
-             Is familiar with world children's literature and it's most prominent authurs and works;
Is familiar with films based on works of world children's literature;
Is able to compare a literary work and a film based on it;
Is able to discuss the aesthetic and ethical value of a literary work or a film, is able to put in words his or her reading and watching experience.

- Student who has completed the course:
- - searches the research article from databases according to the given parameters;
- - analyzes and evaluates professional information;
- - makes a presentation on the basis of a research article;
- - participates in discussions;
- - writes an article on a professional topic for a reader with more general knowledge;
- - works independently according to schedule.

- After this practical course the student:
- -    knows the principles and applications of gravimetric, titrimetric, potentiometric, photometric, (mass)chromatographic (GC, LC, GCMS, LCMS and spectroscopic (atomic, molecular) analysis;
- -    is able to correctly use the basic analytical chemistry instruments and labware (pipettes, volumetric flasks, burettes, balances, magnetic stirrers, heating equipment);
- -    can perform weighing and preparing solutions correctly;
- -    knows the basics of sample preparation in chemical analysis;
- -    knows the concept of selectivity and the main ways of achieving it (separation, masking);
- -    knows the possible sources of uncertainty in analytical methods and ways of minimizing them;
- -    can carry out basic sample preparation for analysis, perform common analytical determinations, critically evaluate the obtained data and calculate the analysis result using MS Excel.

- The student:
- - practises their oral English fluency by means of various task types;
- - selects suitable techniques for developing students' oral self-expression on the two first school stages;
- - compiles and makes an oral (storytelling) presentation;
- - analyses their storytelling presentation;
- - analyses and gives feedback on peers' presentations and sets of techniques.

- After passing the course student
- By the end of this course, students will:
- compare and contrast the varying security dynamics shaping international relations in the Asia-Pacific;
- analyse the influence of China as a rising power on East Asia's regional security dynamics as well as the issues that shape China's relations with high-profile neighbours such as Japan, Taiwan and South Korea; 
- analyse the complicated security situation along the Korean Peninsula;
- distinguish security and integration politics that define a regional organization of rising global importance, ASEAN;
- thoroughly define the position and potential of ASEAN in the context of the wider Asian, Chinese, E.U. and U.S. positions in international affairs;
- write critical, argumentative and scholarly discussion and research papers on important Asia-Pacific security topics.

- After passing the course student
- Upon successful completion of this course, students will: 
Have obtained a detailed knowledge of the main factors that shape the foreign policy formation process, allowing them to apply these throughout their MA studies; 
Have obtained a comprehensive knowledge of the main models proposed to account for decision-making dynamics in the foreign policy formation process, allowing them to apply these throughout their MA studies; 
Have obtained a detailed understanding of the physiological, cognitive and rational-choice approaches regularly applied in the Foreign Policy Analysis (FPA) literature; 
Have gained detailed insights into the linkages between FPA and International Relations Theory;  
Have learned to practically apply models explaining foreign policy decision-making to "real-world" events through a group presentation; 
Have furthered their skills in researching and writing policy memorandums and argumentative scholarly essays.

- The student
- 1) will clearly understand the spoken Võro language on a conversational level;
- 2) will be able to express him/herself in conversation in the Võro language.

- By the end of trainee practice students
- 1) are able to plan, implement, analyze and evaluate their activities related to education in general and EFL teaching in particular;
- 2) are able to plan the teaching process, to teach and to support learners, taking into account the learners' individual characteristics, prior knowledge, needs (including educational needs) and interests;
- 3) design the teaching process systematically and creatively, taking into account the major state regulations and considering the distinctive characteristics of the school, grade and the subject as well as the needs and interests of students;
- 4) integrate successfully the knowledge, skills and attitudes acquired during the studies at the university.

- On completion of the course students will:
- 1.	understand the importance of educational research and dissemination of pedagogical knowledge in the teacher's job;
- 2.	analyze and evaluate the essence of educational research (including research ethics) and the research process;
- 3.	analyze and evaluate the most common research designs as well as data collection and analysis methods in educational research; can apply these in designing and conducting their research;
- 4.	analyze and evaluate research in terms of quality criteria;
- 5.	apply different data analysis software;
- 6.	be able to plan action research.

- On successful completion of the course the students:
- -	Know theoretical approaches and research methods for the study of history of scientific ideas;
- -	Are familiar with some historical examples and can analyse particular cases;
- -	Are able to present results of their analysis and have obtained necessary skills of academic writing.

- Upon completion of the course, the student:
- - has an overview of the topics, skills and attitudes in the field of natural and social studies submitted by the national curriculum to the respective study level and develops his / her skills in the teaching methods necessary for dealing with natural and social studies subjects;
- - is able to compile tasks and demonstration tools in teaching science and social studies.

- A student having passed the course successfully
- -	has basic knowledge on the methodology of web surveys;
- -	knows the central issues and possible solutions of web survey research design;
- -	can apply the acquired knowledge to planning a web survey and setting up a web questionnaire.

- Student who passed the subject:
- 1) knows the leading research trends in modern Russian lexicology and phraseology;
- 2) know the history of the formation of the Russian language vocabulary and the main development trends;
- 3) have a systematic knowledge about the structure of lexical meaning;
- 4) can do a lexical-semantic analysis of the word;
- 5) to be able to assess the area of the use of the word and phraseologism;
- 6) know what information can be found in various dictionaries, orient  in the main electronic  resources (corpora, dictionaries) of the Russian language.

- Upon completing the course, the students will be able to:
- 1) become informed about the European legal integration, the constitutive elements of the EU acquis; and legislative roles of the EU institutions;
- 2) analyse the main sources and principles of EU law, their implementation in the practice and their relation to other general principles of law;
- 3) become informed about treaty-making and constitutional issues of the EU;
- 4) compare and contrast the relationship between the EU law and national law;
- 5) understand and make distinction between different actions in the Court of Justice of the EU; choose the right remedy for a practical case; understand the procedural aspects and interpretation methods of the COJEU;
- 6) analyse a judgment of the COJEU and solve a case in the framework of the basic course according to the given questions/methodology.

- By finishing the course, the student:
-	engages in more complex conversations on topics such as hobbies, work, travel, and daily life using expanded vocabulary and advanced grammar structures;
