- After passing the course student
- 1) Explains the nature of physical geography, its sub-disciplines, and relates them to the astronomical and geophysical foundations of the Earth;


2) Analyzes the structure and functioning of the atmosphere, including radiation and heat balance, movement of air masses, precipitation formation, and the development of the climate system, and applies this knowledge to solving problem situations;


3) Describes the hydrosphere and the water cycle, analyzes the physical properties of water bodies and the components of the water balance, and applies this knowledge to solving problem situations;


4) Explains the nutrient cycles of the biosphere and the spatial distribution of living organisms, characterizes the main biomes and biogeographical patterns;


5) Describes the formation, properties, and distribution of soils, and relates them to environmental conditions and classification principles.

- The student:
