  - Homework: 35 points in total, distributed throughout the first three months of the semester.
  - Project: 35 points in total, to be done during the last month of the semester.
  - Final exam: 30 points (in-person, first half without resources, second part open-book and open-laptop).

To pass the course, you must score at least 51 points in total AND at least 12 out of 30 points in the final exam.

opitulemused_en:
- Student who has completed the course:
- * is able to quickly grasp the content and relevance of a wide range of news, articles, and overviews on specialized topics;
- * is able to give a review of complex texts; is able to summarize and comment on these texts;
- * is able to express him/herself clearly and concisely in written communication;
- * is able to describe, discuss, give an opinion, and comment within the themes to be learned, while using language flexibly and effectively.

- The student:
- - understands the most important concepts and developments regarding data collection and analysis in the digital age;
- - associates the meaning of changes in data collection and its use in the society;
- - understands data development in the improvement of public services in Estonia in the international comparative context;
- - describes data protection developments in Estonia and the European Union, including with regard to young people;
- - explains the importance of data awareness for young people and in the organization;
- - assesses the risks and opportunities associated with data collection and protection in one's  work.

- Student:
- 1. has experience in working in a multicultural environment;
- 2. can communicate with people from other cultures;
- 3. has acquired vocabulary in a specific field in a foreign language environment.

- By the end of the course the student
- - understands terminology and concepts of spatial mobility;
- - is aware how spatial mobility, social mobility and activities of individuals are interrelated;
- - is aware of the impacts of information and communication technologies (ICT) on spatial mobility;
- - knows the data collection methods of spatial mobility;
- - has an overview of passive mobile positioning data;
- - has an overview of active positioning methods and smartphone based data;
- - is able to measure individuals' spatial mobility with mobile positioning;
- - is able to critically analyse the data and highlight the shortcomings of its use;
- - understands the concepts of information society and Smart City;
- - has an overview of social media data and research applications;
- - is able to use mobile phone and social media based information sources for urban studies and planning;
- - is aware of the ethical issues related to the use of big data;
- - understands how culture and context affect the availability and use of data;
- - has an overview of international initiatives in the use of big data;
- - has the ability to work in a group and clearly present the results of the assignments.

- After completing the course a student should be able to:
- read, write and understand simple texts in Russian;
- express oneself choosing the correct vocabulary and grammar learnt during the course;
- get into social contact (to greet, to say goodbye, to introduce oneself, to thank, to apologies etc.);
- talk about family, home town, hobbies, daily routine;  
- understand and apply basic structures of Russian grammar.

- After completing the course, students are able:
- - to formulate and analyze the principles of information architecture in different information environments;
- - understand and critically analyze information organization systems (taxonomy, etc.);
- - apply the principles of information architecture in real information environments;
- - to discuss what is included in the concept of information architecture and other basic concepts in the field;
- - create and describe and make recommendations for the application of information architecture principles, especially when organizing web information.

- After passing the course, the student:
- 1) knows the main stages of Russian history and Russian cultural history;
- 2) has an understanding of the main sources and concepts of Russian history and Russian cultural history;
- 3) can interpret historical events in Russia and analyze the phenomena of Russian culture;
- 4) distinguishes between a scientific and a literary approach to historical facts;
- 5) can use the acquired knowledge in the analysis of works of art.

