opitulemused_en:
- Students completing the Master's Exam:
- 1. have acquired essential knowledge and skills in Data Science;
- 2. are qualified to resolve legal matters in Data Science;
- 3. are qualified to work in a group effectively and in an organised manner.

hindamise_meetod:
Aines kasutatakse pidevhindamist ning iga ülesande eest saab tudeng fikseeritud arvu punkte alljärgneva jaotuse alusel:

1) Tunnikontrollid (9 tk), iga tunnikontroll annab 2p = 18p; 

2) Ajakirjanduslike tekstide kirjutamine igas teemaplokis (4 tk), kus esimesed 2 teksti annavad 10p ja järgmised 2 teksti annavad 11p; 

3) Kirjalik lõputöö, mis annab 40p. 


Kokku on võimalik tudengil ülesannete täitmiselt teenida 100 punkti.

Lõplik tulemus kujuneb täidetud ülesannetelt kogutud punktide summana.

opitulemused_en:
- A student who completed an internship:
- - participates in team work and fulfills assigned tasks;
- - manages the project and the project team;
- - prepares IT project analysis and plans project sustainability;
- - prepares project reporting.

- Upon successful completion of this course, the student:
- 1. is able to find relevant astronomical databases and knows specific properties of astronomical data;
- 2. can make queries to astronomical databases and combine resulting data sets;
- 3. is able to visualise astronomical data and to carry out visual analysis;
- 4. knows main data reduction methods in astronomy, e.g., two-point correlation function, object classification with machine learning, clustering analysis, marked point processes;
- 5. is able to apply data reduction methods in analysis of real astronomical data;
- 6. can use astronomy-specific software tools (Astropy modules in Python, TOPCAT, Aladin);
- 7. understands the importance of observational selection effects in data analysis;
- 8. knows methods for error estimation (for example, bootstrap) and is able to apply them;
- 9. knows main machine learning algorithms and is able to apply them for solving astronomical problems.

- After successfully completing the first part of this course, the student will:
- * have applied the theoretical ideas of state machines in a practical context for solving complex tasks;
- * have controlled numerous robotics platforms;
- * have applied image processing algorithms for solving real-time problems;
- * have implemented a control algorithm for balancing a robot;
- * be familiar with the class of simultaneous localisation and mapping problems;
- * be familiar with the concepts of forward and inverse kinematics;
- * have practiced discussing field-specific topics with other course participants;
- * have practiced developing and testing software on physical robots.

- A student who has participated the course:
- - knows the features of adversarial and inquisitorial procedure and the role that courts, prosecutors and counsels play in it.
- - names features of Packer models and can notice manifestations of these models in contemporary criminal proceedings;
- - analyses requirements for judges, prosecutors and defence counsels in criminal proceedings;
- - analyses the (human) rights of suspects and accused persons that arise at the following stages of criminal proceedings: surveillance, interrogation, arrest and detention, decision to prosecute, and trial;
- - evaluates potential consequences for violation of (human) rights of suspects and accused persons in criminal proceedings.

- Student
- * knows the theory, principles, methodology and legal framework of environmental impact assessment;
- * knows the methodology of Natura assessment;
- * defines and links the roles of different stakeholders in the assessment process;
- * understands the importance of third party involvement and explains the methods of public participation;
- * knows and can apply main methods used in environmental impact assessment; student is able to compare different alternatives using various techniques;
- * assesses the quality of an environmental impact statement in teamwork.

- After completing the course students will be able to
- 1) analyzes the articles on current topics in which the authors present and defend their points of view;
- 2) analyzes the content of different media sources;
- 3) participates in discussion on a topic in his/her area of interest; expresses his/her points of view and brings out arguments for and against different opinions;

- 1) is familiar with the basic theories of translation and their history and is able to compare them with one another;
- 2) distinguishes between grammatical and lexical translation difficulties and is able to solve them;
- 3) is aware of the peculiarities of different texts and their translation (eg, press texts);
- 4) can translate from English into their mother tongue and from mother tongue to English, distinguishing between possible grammatical and lexical difficulties in translation.

- The student
1.	finds information by digital means and assesses its relevance and reliability;
2.	compiles a poster/vlog/ video, etc. in one of the States of the Federal Republic of Germany, using technological means and digital environments for cooperation and content creation;
3.	evaluates his or her own and peers' contribution and development in teamwork;
4.	apply techniques that support the presentation when making a presentation;
5.	analyzes fictional text based of the principles of hermeneutics in the field of linguistic text analysis method;
6.	knows and uses different ways of working with text and different mnemonic techniques to support his/her learning;
7.	knows and uses monolingual electronic dictionaries (dwds.de, duden.de) and language corpora (deSkell) to specity language use:

- The student who has passed the practice:
- - applies the acquired economic knowledge and skills in the work environment and evaluates their achievement;
- - understand the knowledge and skills that need to be developed based on the feedback from the supervisor (s);
- - analyzes the task performed in the internship on a scientific basis;
- - analyzes one's own experience, what has been learned from the experience and plans further development needs based on the aim and tasks of the internship

- By completing the course, the student
- - will understand the basics of transport geography;
- - will understand the social, environmental and health implications of accessibility, transportation and mobility;
- - will have an overview of different types of mobility data and research methods for analysing human mobility and transport flows;
- - will have an overview of contemporary transport solutions and future technologies;
- - will be able to plan the analysis of human mobility needs and opportunities;
- - will understand the role of spatial planning and governance in human mobility-related decision-making and can suggest sustainable mobility solutions to specific contexts;

- Upon successful completion of the course, the students will:
- 1. understand the various stages of the policy process and linkages between them;
- 2. have acquired the basic skills and knowledge for planning and carrying out simple policy analysis;
- 3. be able to distinguish between various genres of policy writing and prepare a position paper;
- 4. know the key theories and models of public policy making and apply these for policy research of their interest.

- After passing the course student
- will have an understanding of the modern Russian literary language*
- distinguish functional styles of the modern Russian language;
- analyze and create texts related to various functional styles;
- classify stylistically marked elements of language;
- will have an idea of the social and regional varieties of the Russian language

- Upon completion of this course, the student is expected to:
- 1. Know the main geophysical and cosmic objects, their location, dimensions and structure;
- 2. Know by order of magnitude the typical values of main physical parameters used to characterize these objects (such as density, pressure or temperature);
- 3. Describe the mechanical, thermal, electromagnetic and chemical processes where these objects are involved in;
- 4. Know the physical  methods providing information about these objects and processes, and be able to estimate the reliability of these methods;
- 5. Describe the chronological evolution of the large-scale physical objects;
- 6. Solve the problems considering the large-scale physical objects within the level of the example problems presented.

- By the end of the course, the student will:
- 1. know the main terms of environmental law and be able to explain the content of the most important principles of environmental law;
- 2. know the connections between environmental law in Estonia, the EU, and at international level;
- 3. have an overview of the main institutions of environmental law and understand their connections to other fields of law;
- 4. know the system of legal means for environmental nuisances and understand its functioning.

- Upon completion of this course, a student will:
- - be aware of social aspects of software testing;
- - select and apply suitable testing techniques within a given software project;
- - apply effective techniques for conflict resolution in a software testing environment;
- - create and defend a software testing plan that takes into account multiple dimensions of software quality.

- Upon successfully passing the course students:
- 1) know what types of public services exist and what is their hierarchy and relation to each other;
- 2) know basic principles of service design and can apply  those principles;
- 3) know the regulations and standards that govern public services and can devise service prototypes that adhere to these;
- 4) can assess which types of data can be collected when providing services and what rules apply to the storage and usage of this data;
- 5) know microservice architecture and its typical development mode, can use this to propose theoretical service prototypes or suggest improvements to existing services;
- 6) know different service governance models and service owner roles and can analyse which models fit to which services in which domains.

- After passing the course student
- A student who has completed the course:

1) is able to explain what datafication means and what kinds of development challenges it presents for society;

2) is familiar with the main topics related to datafication (e.g. at the individual, organizational, and societal levels);

3) is able to critically analyze the potential methodological, theoretical, and interpretive challenges that arise from the use of (big) data.

- By the end of the course the student will:
- - know the structure and functioning of the EU and the basic facts about translating within the EU;
- - recognize the characteristic features of EU texts, select appropriate strategies of translation and editing, and applies required or useful techniques;
- - use appropriate databases and sources while translating EU texts;
- - perform translating tasks individually and as a member of a team and manage their time accordingly

- Students who have completed the course:
- Will be able to analyse aspects of the linguistic structure of Estonian and compare them with other languages;
- Will have gained experience in discussing empirical linguistic research;
- Will have a good understanding of the structure and contents of research papers in linguistics and linguistics terminology;
- Will be able to situate their own research plans in the context of earlier research on structural aspects of Estonian.

- After passing the course student
- Upon completion of the course, the student

1. Describes informally the fundamental notions of quantum information: qubit and entanglement;

2. Explains the basic quantum communication protocols like quantum key distribution;

3. Uses a method to tabularize known properties of collections of qubits and track them over time as the qubits are being processed;

4. Explains how processing of quantum information is described using the Heisenberg formalism;

5. Describes the quantum teleportation protocol using the tabularization method.

- Upon successful completion of the course the student:
- knows basic construction systems used in stages;
- can find solutions to attach more common constructions;
- can independently construct simpler objects;
