opitulemused_en:
- Upon successful completion of the course, the student will be able to:
- 1. understand and know the principles of the functioning of international systems in international military-political, economic and socio-cultural relations, as well as understand the impact of the natural environment on international relations;
- 2. interpret events in international politics at various levels of analysis;
- 3. see the link between theoretical interpretations and actual world politics;
- 4. assess developments in world politics;
- 5. perceive the roles and functions of international organisations in the system of international relations.
- During the course, students will develop various skills - teamwork, oral and written expression, data retrieval and use - needed to complete the various tasks in the course.

- Upon completing the course a student:
- - knows the principles of mobility and transport planning at both local and regional level;
- - understands the concepts of accessibility, mobility and transport, analysis methods and their role in spatial planning;
- - knows the principles of sustainable mobility and human scale urban planning and is able to apply them in a specific context;
- - is able to link mobility planning to the to the environment, human health and well-being and social inequalities;
- - is able to work with planning documents, have skills in analyzing and compiling mobility plans.

- After successful completion of the course, the student will:
- skillfully apply various methodologies, teaching methods, and techniques in teaching physics;
- analyze techniques for motivating learners to achieve the learning outcomes set out in the national curriculum;
- use various demonstration techniques competently in physics lessons;
- apply techniques for planning experiments, tests, and practical work;
- be able to solve and compile tasks independently, based on learning objectives and taking into account the level of learners;
- reflect on own actions as a physics teacher and work on self-development.

- The student will: 

understand the central ideas and concepts of J. v. Uexküll, J. Lotman and other scholars of Tartu semiotics; 

relate these ideas with their further developments and interpret them contextually; 

describe and analyse the application of the ideas and their changes; 

comprehend the impact of these thinkers on the development of semiotic theory 

acquire skills of academic discussion and group work.

- The students, who have passed this course:
- *acquire the basics of ancient Greek morphology (incl. irregular verb forms), are able to decline nouns and adjectives from different declinations and conjugate verbs in all tenses and moods;
- * are able to form and decline the participles, infinitives and verbal adjectives, give them a meaning in the text;
- *are able to identify different parts of speech and sentence, comment different word-forms;
- *have acquired basics of ancient Greek syntax, analyze simple texts from the syntactical aspect;
- *form sentences or short text-segments using the acquired knowledge of morphology, syntax and basic vocabulary;
- * have general knowledge about the history  of ancient Greek literature (including philosophy and history writers) thanks to the choice of manual texts;
- *are able to use the dictionaries and grammars;
- *are able to find sources in the internet, know the most important internet and digital sources for translating and commenting texts.

- As a result of successful studies of the Observation- and Pedagogical Practice course the student will:
- - be able to observe and analyze the daily work of the teacher;
- - be able to analyze students' learning activities in classrooms;
- - be able to plan and conduct lessons or part of lessons with the help of the teacher;
- - be able to analyze your own activities as a teacher (teacher assistant) in the classroom.

